00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:17.696
So in today's
episode, we're going

00:00:17.696 --> 00:00:20.360
to talk about a critical part
of any business, protection

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:21.680
and management of data.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:23.570
And mismanagement of
sensitive information

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:24.535
can come at all costs.

00:00:24.535 --> 00:00:25.910
A.T. MCWILLIAMS:
The challenge is

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:28.040
how to protect sensitive
data while still being

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:30.140
able to use it for important
business functions,

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.630
like running analytics or
customer support operations.

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:35.171
HEATHER CROSS: Well, we've got
an answer for this, Cloud Data

00:00:35.171 --> 00:00:36.030
Loss Prevention API.

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:38.750
We'll also run through a quick
demo of the self-paced lab

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:40.880
where we'll set up
a DLP API and use

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.759
it to inspect a string of data
for sensitive information.

00:00:47.841 --> 00:00:50.090
A.T. MCWILLIAMS: So when it
comes to properly handling

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:53.040
sensitive data, the first step
is knowing where it exists.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.790
Well, the DLP API uses
a variety of techniques

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:57.830
to identify sensitive
data like credit card

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:00.200
numbers, social
security numbers, names,

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.031
and other forms of personally
identifiable information.

00:01:03.031 --> 00:01:05.239
In addition to identifying
sensitive data within text

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:08.330
content, the API can detect
and classify sensitive data

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:10.482
within standard
bitmap images too.

00:01:10.482 --> 00:01:12.440
HEATHER CROSS: Applying
the API to tech streams

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:14.150
allows you to
automatically redact

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:17.390
or censor any sensitive data
before it writes to disk,

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:20.600
generates logs, or is used
to perform analysis tasks.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:22.400
Identifying sensitive
data upfront

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.370
also allows you to choose the
most suitable storage system

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:27.770
and appropriate access
controls for that data.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:35.650
The DLP API classifies
raw data by using

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:39.040
a set of predefined detectors
to identify patterns, formats,

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:40.300
and checksums.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.175
The API can even understand
contextual clues.

00:01:42.175 --> 00:01:44.508
A.T. MCWILLIAMS: Once you
know where your sensitive data

00:01:44.508 --> 00:01:47.770
lives, the API gives you the
option to deidentify that data.

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:48.840
So what does that mean?

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.370
Deidentifying data removes
identifying information

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:54.250
from a dataset, making it
more difficult to associate

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.230
the remaining data
with an individual

00:01:56.230 --> 00:01:58.060
and reducing the
risk of exposure.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:00.700
The resulting data is then
suitable for apps, storage,

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:01.810
or analysis.

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:04.750
HEATHER CROSS: Redaction removes
entire values or entire records

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:05.860
from a dataset.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.020
Partial masking hides
parts of the data,

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:09.550
leaving some data visible--

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:11.050
for example, the
last seven digits

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:12.520
of a US telephone number.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.590
A.T. MCWILLIAMS: Tokenization,
or secure hashing,

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:16.640
replaces sensitive
data with a key.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.820
This is often used in
credit card processing.

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:21.640
Dynamic data masking applies
deidentification and masking

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:23.209
techniques in real time.

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:25.750
This can be useful if you don't
want to alter your underlying

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:28.414
data but want to mask it
when viewed by certain users.

00:02:28.414 --> 00:02:29.830
Special access
privileges could be

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:31.413
applied for those
who need to view it,

00:02:31.413 --> 00:02:33.610
say HR or a finance team.

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:35.710
HEATHER CROSS: The DLP
API integrates with many

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:37.859
of our other GCP services too.

00:02:37.859 --> 00:02:39.400
Cloud Functions can
help you automate

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:40.774
the classification
of data that's

00:02:40.774 --> 00:02:42.250
uploaded to Cloud Storage.

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:45.130
Also, there's a built-in support
for scanning and classifying

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:48.580
sensitive data and Cloud
Storage, Cloud Datastore,

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:49.570
and BigQuery.

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:52.000
Cloud Pub/Sub notifications
can be generated in response

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.530
to a completed inspection jobs.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:00.530
We then created
a service account

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:03.710
to access the Google Cloud
APIs when testing locally

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:06.620
and gave the service account
the required permissions.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:10.010
We then generated a
service account key.

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:13.740
So first, we create a JSON file
with sensitive information.

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:16.090
In this case, it's
a phone number.

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:17.950
We authenticate to
our service account,

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:21.010
